  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe large-scale pattern will be governed by varying forms of \nvicinity ridging through the weekend, and into the first half of \nnext week. At present, the prior Rex Block has unlocked and is being \nreplaced by a weak mid-level shortwave trough getting pushed \ndownstream of Upper Midwest ridging. This should provide ambient \nascent over Lower Michigan, generating healthy coverage of clouds \naloft. Thermal fields remain intact, with 850 mb temperatures \nholding near 10C which keeps highs at least 5F below climatological \nnormals. Strong stability within the lower half of the column, \npositioned within a well-established anticyclone ensures subsidence \nand limited boundary-layer cloud.\n\nRain-free weather extends through the weekend with a warming trend \nlifting readings toward early Fall averages. An elongated ridge axis \nextending from the Gulf into Ontario, subdivided by southern and \nnorthern stream components, will move eastward by Saturday. The \nnorthern stream shorter wavelength feature displaces the residual \ntroughing, and centers over the western Great Lakes. High pressure \nwill lock in at the surface, centering between southwest Lower and \nQuebec, offering light winds from the northeast quadrant. Meanwhile, \na strong Atlantic coast quasi-tropical system/nor'easter will \nmeander between Chesapeake Bay and Cape Cod over the next 3+ days. \nThe upper levels of this system and the stationary mature low are \nwhat will prevent much movement from the composite ridge axis over \nthe Great Lakes region. A strong positively-tilted Pacific longwave \ntrough eventually digs across CONUS mid-week, kicking out the \nAtlantic wave. High confidence in a dry forecast prior to the \narrival of this system, once pre-frontal winds flip southerly and \ntop-down column saturation gets underway closer to Wednesday.",
